body=f'''<div class="eyebrow">Full strategy review / 26 October 2026</div><h1>Every rule.<br>Evidence and limits.</h1><p class="lead">SS45DTE remains a manual research playbook. The corrected five-name benchmark lost <strong>$3,002</strong>. These tests do not establish a dependable trading edge or validate discretionary judgment.</p>
<div class="notice" id="correction"><strong>Correction to the earlier +$293 headline.</strong> The old implementation omitted IV-history observations while holding a position. Continuous daily history changes its 25 trades / +$293 into <strong>18 trades / −$3,002</strong>, with 5.2% account drawdown. All older receipts remain accessible as historical diagnostics. The research source now contains the fix and a regression test.</div>
<h2 id="results">Final comparisons on corrected IV history</h2><p>2 Jan 2024–23 Oct 2026 · $150,000 initial equity · same modeled costs. Click a row for its QC backtest; login may be required.</p>{ctable}<p class="small">The control uses one pair per entry and disables stress sizing/loss exits; the pilot restores proposed stress limits. These are full-policy comparisons: holdings, event windows, later entries and capital availability change. The 17-name control is not the stricter pilot. This is not a matched-trade causal experiment or independent validation. The capped pilot contains only six TLT trades; its QC-reported 0.000% drawdown must not be read as zero risk or a guaranteed loss limit.</p>
<h2>Review of every component</h2>{ledger}
<h2 id="filters">Why so few trades?</h2>{funnel}<p>Counts are unique underlying/session stage passes, not minute retries or independent trade opportunities. The code checks IVR before DTE, so IVR losses above include days outside the monthly entry window. In the unchanged earlier run, calendar-first masks found <strong>1,009 eligible name-days → 224 after IVR</strong>: 77.8% removed within the window. That mask is specific to the earlier 25-trade run.</p><p>In the corrected run, OI removes 66 of 141 daily-delta candidate-days; spreads remove 42 of 71 quote-ready dates; live delta removes 11 of 29 survivors. EWZ and SWKS still have no trades. Their quote-ready candidates all fail the 5% spread gate. The program picks one pair near 0.19 delta before checking live liquidity; it does not search all acceptable pairs.</p>
<h2 id="costs">Low profit is not merely idle capital</h2><p>The corrected baseline’s 11 winners earn <strong>$4,173</strong>; seven losers lose <strong>$7,175</strong>. Mean win <strong>$379.36</strong>, mean loss <strong>$1,025</strong>, profit factor <strong>0.58</strong>. Net per pair is <strong>−$166.78</strong>, and the largest realized pair loss is <strong>$4,605</strong>. More contracts would magnify these outcomes.</p><p>In the corrected run, modeled half-spread cost is $800, fees $72 and slippage $72. Even hypothetical cost-free midpoint execution would lose $2,058. This arithmetic is not a fill simulation. The earlier +$293 ledger also illustrates execution fragility: $938.50 of modeled half-spread cost, $100 fees and $100 slippage. Another $0.03/share of adverse slippage on its 100 standard-contract fills would cost $300 and turn it into −$7. This is fixed-ledger arithmetic, not another backtest and not the corrected-series P&amp;L.</p>
<h2 id="universe">A wider watchlist is not a larger risk budget</h2><p>The original list—CRWD, EWZ, META, TLT, SWKS—came from prior data coverage, with just three names producing trades. The expanded list adds SPY, QQQ, IWM, DIA, EFA, EEM, XLF, XLE, XLV, GLD, SLV and HYG. It broadens opportunity types, but overlapping US-equity ETFs are correlated and one contract does not equalize risk.</p><p>The corrected 17-name run produces <strong>{broad['reconciliation']['episodes']} pairs</strong>, <strong>{fmt(broad['reconciliation']['net_pnl'])}</strong> net and <strong>{broad['statistics']['Drawdown']}</strong> drawdown. It triggered the 10% drawdown circuit breaker and halted new entries; its 62 trades therefore include the effect of that halt. Its five-position capacity and original-name-first ordering also affect which trades occur. Expand the research watchlist; do not infer that every new candidate fits the 1% stress pilot.</p>
<h2>Where the corrected baseline earns and loses</h2>{name_table}{annual}<p class="small">Year results allocate whole realized trade P&amp;L to the exit year; they are not annual marked-equity returns. These are descriptive slices of the same selected sample, not independent validation or a reason to discard losing names after seeing outcomes.</p><h2 id="timing">Entry and exit clocks</h2><p>The corrected sample’s entry DTE range is 41–50, median 46. Its holding duration ranges 12–29 calendar days, median 21. There are ten scheduled exits and eight earnings exits. The 14/7-DTE variants retain the same other filters but can change subsequent entries; they do not establish that a particular exit is optimal. Holidays and half sessions move scheduled exits earlier; new entries stop 35 days before the test ends.</p>
<h2>What automation can and cannot do</h2><p>QC can automate the specified selection, entry, sizing, exit and accounting rules; these completed cloud runs demonstrate that. The hard part is establishing an economic edge with realistic data and execution, and turning contextual judgment into decisions recorded before outcomes are known. Trading by hand does not itself fix negative expectancy. Manual execution can simplify operations and improve event checks, but its added value still requires measurement.</p><h2 id="manual">How to make manual discretion measurable</h2><ol><li>Before acting, record entry, skip, hold, reduce or close; the exact contracts; the reason; the data snapshot; and the baseline closing date.</li><li>Link actual broker fills and fees to that decision. Save skips as well as trades so selection is not judged only after favorable outcomes.</li><li>Compare with a frozen 45→21 baseline. Separate candidate selection, execution-price improvement and early-exit effects; report missing counterfactual quotes.</li></ol><p><a href="manual_decisions_template.csv">Download the compact decision-log template</a>. The template does not store or submit orders.</p>
<h2 id="limits">What is still not established</h2><p>Optimal entry DTE, delta band, OI threshold, IVR threshold, profit-taking rule and delta alert; reliable annual income; calibrated tail probabilities; full-window point-in-time earnings exclusion; live limit-fill performance; or the value of discretionary judgment. The tested universe is static and surviving, and all comparisons share inspected data. The 2024–2026 sample excludes crises such as 2008 and 2020. Intraday bar-based fills do not establish multi-leg limit-order fill probability, market impact or assignment resilience.</p><p>The replay estimates ATM IV near 30 DTE and computes IVR = 100 × (current IV − rolling minimum)/(rolling maximum − rolling minimum), using up to 252 observations with at least 60. It is not the selected 45-DTE OTM legs’ own IV history or IV percentile. QC daily Greeks are prior-session observations; the live delta/stress approximation uses BSM, a 4% rate and zero dividends. This differs from American-option and dividend-aware broker models. Finite ±20% spot / +10,+20 volatility-point stresses do not bound naked-call losses. Missing current quotes, event visibility or account capacity cannot be treated as a pass.</p>
<h2>Next evidence, in priority order</h2><ol><li>Capture fresh exact-leg quotes, broker capacity and full holding-window events for every paper decision. Keep skipped candidates.</li><li>Freeze the manual specification and record a prospective paper sample. Reconcile actual executable prices and fees, rather than assuming midpoint improvement.</li><li>Obtain complete historical contracts/events and test a separate period including severe volatility shocks. Evaluate assignment and realistic limit fills before changing the risk budget.</li><li>Only then compare alternatives on the same entry opportunities and risk budget. Do not select a winning parameter from the already inspected 2024–2026 sample.</li></ol><h2>Independent sources: what they do and do not support</h2><p><a href="https://www.tastylive.com/news-insights/How-Capital-Allocation-Impacts-Volatility">Tastylive’s 2024 SPY study</a> compares 45-DTE one-standard-deviation strangles held to expiry, managed at 50% profit, or closed at 21 DTE. It reports favorable volatility control for 21-DTE exits at 25% and 35% capital allocation. Different universe, sizing and implementation mean it supports a research hypothesis, not replication of our results.</p><p><a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr736.html">New York Fed research on variance-risk pricing</a> provides economic context for selling volatility risk; it does not validate our particular strikes, filters, sizing or 45→21 policy. <a href="https://www.optionseducation.org/strategies/all-strategies/short-strangle">OIC’s short-strangle description</a> documents limited premium, unbounded upside loss and adverse exposure to rising IV.</p><p><a href="https://www.quantconnect.com/docs/v2/writing-algorithms/universes/equity-options">QC documents prior-day universe Greeks</a>; <a href="https://www.quantconnect.com/docs/v2/writing-algorithms/datasets/eod-historical-data/upcoming-earnings">its earnings feed documents seven-day coverage</a>. These implementation limits matter when comparing research with manual decisions.</p><h2>Receipts and reproducibility</h2><p><a href="review_evidence_20261026.json">All run statistics, funnels, reconciliations and hashes</a> · <a href="review_trades_20261026.csv">Trade-by-trade ledger</a> · <a href="review_report_20261026.txt">Full review</a> · <a href="review_episode_metrics_20261026.json">Entry/exit and payoff distributions</a> · <a href="review_sources_20261026.json">Source manifest</a> · <a href="current_control.py.txt">Current control source</a> · <a href="current_pilot.py.txt">Current pilot source</a>.</p><p>The first corrected-pilot attempt reported a fatal runtime error despite a flat ledger; it is excluded from completed comparisons. Its identical-code rerun is shown above. The unchanged instrumented baseline reproduces all 100 original order identities, times, quantities and prices. Every published result reconciles native option fills to equity with no residual positions. Thirteen targeted tests cover risk units, signed margin, original entry basis, daily IV history, calendar handling and lifecycle recovery.</p><p><a href="strategy.html">Read the manual strategy page ↗</a> · <a href="qc.html">Archived 25 Oct QC evidence ↗</a></p>'''
(W/'review.html').write_text(page('Full quantitative review',body,'review.html'))
body=f'''<div class="eyebrow">Manual strategy / SS45DTE</div><h1>45 DTE in.<br>21 DTE out.</h1><p class="lead">A short-strangle playbook for trading by hand: choose a qualified opportunity, keep the position small, and close the whole pair by the planned date.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Research stage.</strong> The corrected benchmark lost $3,002; the current capped pilot produced {fmt(pilot['reconciliation']['net_pnl'])} across {pilot['reconciliation']['episodes']} pairs. Neither establishes a dependable edge. Use this as a paper/manual research protocol, with fixed risk limits and a decision log. <a href="review.html">Full quantitative review ↗</a></div>
<div class="cycle"><div><b>45 DTE</b><span>CHOOSE THE MONTHLY PAIR</span></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div><b>21 DTE</b><span>CLOSE AND RECONCILE</span></div><div class="arrow">→</div><div><b>Review</b><span>NEXT MONTH IS A NEW DECISION</span></div></div>
<h2>The manual checklist</h2><ol><li><strong>Choose the cycle.</strong> Target 45 calendar DTE, allowing 40–50 as a recorded tolerance. Choose a standard monthly expiry and write down its 21-DTE closing date. Use the preceding full trading session for holidays or half sessions.</li><li><strong>Qualify the opportunity.</strong> Same-source IVR >50, with its lookback and observation count recorded; no known company earnings in the entire planned holding window; current quotes and account capacity. The backtest’s seven-day earnings feed is insufficient for that full-window check.</li><li><strong>Select the actual legs.</strong> One out-of-the-money put and one call, same expiry and equal quantity. Reference |delta| 0.16–0.22 per leg; positive bids, OI >500, spread &lt;5% of midpoint per leg. Compare eligible strikes using current quotes; do not assume the first theoretical pair is executable.</li><li><strong>One pair or skip.</strong> The proposed paper pilot caps trade/cluster modeled stress at 1% of equity and total strategy stress at 2%; maximum two pairs open, one per underlying. New-pair margin ≤5% and total account margin ≤20%. These are risk choices, not proven optimal limits. Retain 0.5% if that is your chosen allowance, and skip pairs that do not fit.</li><li><strong>Execute and review manually.</strong> Use a limit order, verify both fills and record costs. Recheck near the open and before the close. A failed morning attempt may be reconsidered later if every check still passes. No averaging up or rescue rolling.</li><li><strong>Close by 21 DTE.</strong> Earlier exits are permitted for risk or a recorded discretionary reason. Confirm both legs are flat before considering the next monthly pair. Replacement is a fresh entry, never an obligation to stay invested.</li></ol>
<h2>Keep judgment inside measurable boundaries</h2><div class="columns"><div><span class="tag">Quantitative</span><p>Expiry, exit date, current quotes, IVR definition, leg deltas/OI, stress loss, margin, contract count, actual fills and net P&amp;L.</p></div><div><span class="tag">Discretionary</span><p>Choose or skip a qualified candidate; interpret news; choose among liquid strikes; set a limit; hold, reduce or exit early after an alert. Record the reason at the time.</p></div></div>
<h2>Hard boundaries and review alerts</h2><p>Under the proposed pilot, close/reduce when actual marked loss or cumulative scenario loss exceeds 1% of equity, group/book stress exceeds its limit, or account margin exceeds 20%. At 10% below the account’s running peak, close strategy exposure and halt new entries pending review. These instructions cannot guarantee fill prices or cap gap losses.</p><p>|delta| >0.35 and 50% net-credit capture are <strong>review alerts</strong>, not proven automatic exit rules. They cannot delay a hard risk or expiry exit. Stress uses original net entry credit throughout the position, not a reset to today’s midpoint.</p><p><strong>Before entry, write:</strong> why this trade, what would invalidate it, and why one pair fits the risk budget. Save the decision and actual fills. <a href="manual_decisions_template.csv">Decision-log template ↗</a></p>
<p><a href="sizing.html">Sizing calculation and scenario grid ↗</a> · <a href="review.html#results">Corrected comparisons ↗</a> · <a href="review.html#filters">Which filters remove candidates ↗</a> · <a href="data.html">Data-provider gaps ↗</a></p><p class="small">Scope: standard US stock/ETF options. A naked short call has theoretically unlimited loss. A finite stress budget is not a guaranteed maximum loss. CN, futures and index-option variants require separate specifications.</p>'''
(W/'strategy.html').write_text(page('Manual SS45DTE strategy',body,'strategy.html'))
